# 64-bit build (milestone 12, way A): the own work of a class derived from a
# pool base reached through a byte offset. On the GameCube it starts at the
# size of the base; on LP64 where the compiler puts the derived class's first
# member: at the end of the base's data (cEsp: 0x10C of 0x110, tail padding
# reused) for a member aligned to 4 or less, 8-aligned (0x110) for one that
# holds a pointer (RE4_OWN(B, T, gc) in re4_whole_prelude.h, T the member's
# type or one of its alignment). Found with port/tools/m12/raw_offset_check.py
# (offsets past the end of the record).
# - r117.cpp (the church): the light beams are done when bit 2 of byte 0xF9 is
#   set. The beam is an esp06 (the effect that rides a path; ESP06_WK.Flg bit 2:
#   stopped at the path end, the only effect that sets bit 2 of its work's byte
#   1). ESP06_WK holds a pointer (pPath), so on LP64 its work starts at 0x110 and
#   the byte is 0x111. Until 19.10.2026 this patch read 0x10D (the work start of
#   a u8): the puzzle's check of the pattern waited for the beam forever (tester,
#   POCO X7 Pro, 64 bit, r117: the game ran on, the picture and the sound stood).
#   The start of every effect class's work measured with the build's flags: 9
#   of 36 begin at 0x110 on LP64 (esp03 06 0d 0e 11 12 16 3f 4e).
# (esp_sub.cpp's spline sprite, the same case, is patch 0040.)
--- a/src/st1/r117.cpp
+++ b/src/st1/r117.cpp
@@ -81,7 +81,10 @@
 {
     int on = 0;
 
-    if (((u8*) esp)[0xF9] & 4) {
+    // port: patch 0077, byte 1 of the beam's own work: Flg of ESP06_WK (esp06.cpp, the effect that
+    // rides a path; bit 2: stopped at the end), 0xF9 on the GameCube. On LP64 that work starts
+    // 8-aligned behind cEsp (it holds a pointer, pPath): 0x110, not at cEsp's data end 0x10C
+    if (((u8*) esp)[RE4_OWN(cEsp, void*, 0xF8) + 1] & 4) {
         on = 1;
     }
     return on;
